WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.680
Take a mouse with no working rods or cones.

2
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.460
It still responds to light.

3
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:12.700
Its body clock still shifts with day and night, and its pupils still narrow in

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:16.100
bright light. These are non -image -forming responses.

5
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.080
They report how bright the world is, not what is in it.

6
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.140
So which cells detect the light?

7
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.960
A small set of retinal ganglion cells.

8
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.300
They carry their own light pigment, called melanopsin.

9
00:00:29.083 --> 00:00:34.143
These cells are called intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells, or

10
00:00:34.143 --> 00:00:40.023
ipRGCs. An ipRGC makes its own light response with melanopsin.

11
00:00:40.683 --> 00:00:44.723
It also relays signals that rods and cones send through bipolar cells.

12
00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:46.463
So it has two jobs.

13
00:00:46.923 --> 00:00:49.703
Hold on to that. The rest of the film depends on it.

14
00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:51.407
Now a harder question.

15
00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:54.707
Do these cells also help with seeing patterns?

16
00:00:55.407 --> 00:00:59.907
Mice lacking rod and cone signalling were able to discriminate a grating from an

17
00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:01.047
equally bright grey.

18
00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:06.387
Cells labelled with a melanopsin reporter heavily innervated a brain target, the

19
00:01:06.387 --> 00:01:11.747
dLGN. So these cells give a route for very coarse pattern information.

20
00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:13.747
It is not normal acuity.

21
00:01:13.747 --> 00:01:17.587
Those mice manage 0 .16 cycles per degree.

22
00:01:18.187 --> 00:01:20.647
Wild type mice manage 0 .55.

23
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.960
The second result comes from a different experiment.

24
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.180
Mice lacking melanopsin showed deficits in contrast sensitivity.

25
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:30.380
Both results come from mice.

26
00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:32.120
That sounds like the answer.

27
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.060
But look at what was removed.

28
00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.680
Taking out the pigment removes one job.

29
00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.860
Taking out the cells removes both.

30
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.160
Now picture an ablation.

31
00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.700
Remove the cells, and the animal does worse.

32
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.300
You have lost the melanopsin response and the rod and cone relay together.

33
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.080
The experiment cannot say which one mattered.

34
00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.300
Ablation implicates the cells.

35
00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.120
It does not name the input.

36
00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.100
A task can fail without the pigment mattering.

37
00:01:58.783 --> 00:01:59.843
So how do you decide?

38
00:02:00.903 --> 00:02:05.023
Compare melanopsin knockouts with animals whose ipRGCs are removed.

39
00:02:05.403 --> 00:02:10.303
Prefer an adult, timed manipulation, because loss during development can rewire

40
00:02:10.303 --> 00:02:12.463
targets. And remember the limits.

41
00:02:12.963 --> 00:02:17.723
These results come from mice, and grating discrimination is not normal acuity.

42
00:02:18.423 --> 00:02:21.743
Removing the cells and removing the pigment answer different questions.
